(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-histology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Histology — Anatomy Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What are the two main components of cartilage matrix, and is cartilage vascular?","choices":["Mostly fibers and ground substance; cartilage is avascular and not innervated","Composed mostly of minerals; cartilage tissue is described as highly vascularized","Mostly fluid; cartilage heals rapidly thanks to an abundant blood supply","Mostly cells; cartilage is richly innervated with pain and pressure fibers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cartilage matrix is mostly fibers and ground substance, and cartilage is avascular and not innervated, making it slow to heal. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the perichondrium, and what limits cartilage thickness?","choices":["Surrounding tissue from which nutrients diffuse and chondrocytes form; diffusion must cross the entire thickness, limiting how thick cartilage can be","A vascular membrane that directly supplies blood to embedded chondrocytes within the matrix","A mineral layer embedded within the matrix that actively prevents any further outward cartilage growth from occurring","A bony covering surrounding and reinforcing the cartilage matrix from the outside, continuous with periosteum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The perichondrium is the surrounding tissue from which nutrients diffuse and where chondrocytes form; because diffusion must cross the entire thickness of the cartilage, this limits how thick cartilage can become. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are the three types of cartilage described in the material?","choices":["Hyaline, fibrocartilage, and elastic","Dense regular, dense irregular, and areolar","Simple, stratified, and transitional","Compact, spongy, and reticular"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three types of cartilage are hyaline, fibrocartilage, and elastic. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is hyaline cartilage's structure and where is it found?","choices":["Contains mostly elastin fibers, giving it recoil; forms the pinna of the ear and the epiglottis","Mostly mineralized ground substance similar to bone; found exclusively within the flat bones of the skull","Tough and flexible with more matrix/fewer cells, serving as a shock absorber; covers ends of long bones and forms the \"skeleton\" of the trachea and bronchi","Less firm with more cells and fibers, resembling dense connective tissue; forms the intervertebral disks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hyaline cartilage is tough and flexible with more matrix and fewer cells, serving as a shock absorber; it covers the ends of long bones as articular cartilage and forms the \"skeleton\" of the trachea and bronchi. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is fibrocartilage's structure and where is it found?","choices":["Contains mostly elastin fibers with high recoil; forms the pinna of the ear and the epiglottis","The most abundant cartilage type in the body, forming the tracheal and bronchial skeletal framework","Less firm than hyaline, with more cells and fibers, similar in structure to dense regular tissue (tendon); forms intervertebral disks and knee menisci","Tough and flexible, acting as the primary shock absorber within joints; it covers the articulating ends of long bones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Fibrocartilage is less firm than hyaline cartilage, with more cells and fibers, structurally similar to dense regular tissue (tendon); it forms a transitional tissue between tendon and articular hyaline cartilage, and forms the intervertebral disks and spongy knee menisci. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is elastic cartilage's composition and where is it found?","choices":["Contains mostly calcium hydroxyapatite; forms the vertebrae","Contains mostly reticular fibers; forms lymph nodes","Contains mostly ground substance with few fibers; forms the trachea","Contains more elastin fibers; forms the ear pinna and epiglottis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Elastic cartilage contains more elastin fibers than the other cartilage types, and forms structures like the ear pinna and the epiglottis. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which type of cartilage is described as the most abundant type in the body?","choices":["Elastic cartilage","Reticular cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Hyaline cartilage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hyaline cartilage is described as the most abundant type of cartilage in the body. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does bone matrix mostly consist of, and what percentage of bone weight is calcium hydroxyapatite?","choices":["Mostly calcium and phosphate; 65% of bone weight is calcium hydroxyapatite","Composed mostly of collagen protein; roughly 65% of bone weight is attributed to collagen fibers","Mostly water; bone contains no hydroxyapatite","Mostly ground substance; 25% of bone weight is hydroxyapatite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bone matrix is mostly calcium and phosphate, and 65% of bone weight is calcium hydroxyapatite (calcium phosphate, calcium hydroxide, calcium carbonate). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Is bone tissue vascular and innervated, unlike cartilage?","choices":["Yes — bone is highly vascular and well innervated, and contains lymph channels","No — bone is avascular and uninnervated, like cartilage","Bone is vascular but not innervated, unlike the surrounding cartilage tissue","Bone is innervated but avascular, unlike the surrounding cartilage tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Unlike cartilage, bone is highly vascular and well innervated, and it contains lymph channels. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What are the two functions of bone tissue related to mineral and blood, per the material?","choices":["Mineral storage and impulse transmission","Fat digestion and mineral storage","Mineral storage and blood cell production","Blood cell production and lubrication of joints"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bone tissue functions in mineral storage and blood cell production. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the Haversian system (osteon), and what does the Haversian canal contain?","choices":["A specialized type of cartilage located in the epiphysis; it is populated by chondrocytes","The fibrous outer covering of bone; it contains only lymphatic vessels and no blood supply","A specialized type of bone marrow; it is populated by hematopoietic stem cells","The structural unit of compact bone; the Haversian canal contains blood vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Haversian system (osteon) is a structural unit of bone histology, and its central Haversian canal contains blood vessels. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are lamellae, in bone histology?","choices":["The canals connecting osteocytes to each other","The outer fibrous covering of bone","Concentric rings of hard bone","Small spaces housing osteocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lamellae are concentric rings of hard bone, part of the Haversian system's structure. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are canaliculi, in bone histology?","choices":["The outer fibrous covering of bone, continuous with the tendon attachments","Concentric rings of hard bone matrix surrounding the central Haversian canal","Connecting tunnels linking osteocytes in lacunae to each other and to the Haversian canal","The thin membrane lining the inner surface of the bone, facing the marrow cavity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Canaliculi are the connecting tunnels that link osteocytes in their lacunae to each other and to the Haversian canal. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the difference between the periosteum and endosteum?","choices":["Periosteum and endosteum both refer only to cartilage","Periosteum lines the internal surfaces of bone, while endosteum forms the outer covering","Both terms are said to describe the exact identical fibrous covering layer of bone tissue","Periosteum is the outer covering of bone; endosteum lines the internal bone surfaces"],"answer":3,"exp":"The periosteum is the outer covering of bone, while the endosteum lines the internal (inner) bone surfaces. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the difference between compact (cortical) bone and trabecular (cancellous) bone?","choices":["Both terms describe the identical dense outer bone structure with Haversian systems","Compact bone forms the spongy internal lattice, while trabecular bone forms the dense outer shell","Compact bone is dense outer bone with Haversian systems; trabecular bone is the internal, lattice-like spongy bone","Compact and trabecular bone are both said to refer only to types of hyaline cartilage tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Compact (cortical) bone is the dense outer bone tissue containing Haversian systems, while trabecular (cancellous) bone is the internal, lattice-like spongy bone. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Where is red marrow found, and what is its function?","choices":["Contains osteoclasts only, functioning in bone resorption","Contains cartilage-forming chondrocytes","Contains only fat and functions as an energy store","Contains hematopoietic tissue and produces blood cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Red marrow contains hematopoietic tissue and produces blood cells. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What are the zones of the epiphyseal (growth) plate in a long bone, in order?","choices":["Only two zones exist: resting and calcification","Zone of ossification, zone of resting cartilage, zone of proliferation, zone of hypertrophy","Zone of calcification, zone of hypertrophy, zone of proliferation, zone of resting cartilage","Zone of resting cartilage, zone of proliferation, zone of hypertrophy, zone of calcification"],"answer":3,"exp":"The epiphyseal plate's zones, in order from the epiphysis toward the diaphysis, are the zone of resting cartilage, zone of proliferation, zone of hypertrophy, and zone of calcification. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What happens to chondrocytes as they progress through the growth plate zones?","choices":["They divide and stack on top of one another, then die upon calcification as blood vessels from the diaphysis grow into the area","They remain entirely static within the resting zone of the growth plate and never divide throughout the process","They transform directly into osteoclasts that resorb the surrounding calcified cartilage matrix","They immediately die upon entering the resting zone of the growth plate, before any proliferation occurs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Chondrocytes divide and stack on top of one another as they progress through the growth plate, then die upon calcification, at which point blood vessels from the diaphysis grow into the area. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"With what structures is muscle tissue associated, per the material?","choices":["Only the central nervous system's supporting glial structures","Only the walls of blood vessels and lymphatic channels","The bones of the skeleton, the heart, and the walls of hollow organs","Only the bones of the axial and appendicular skeleton"],"answer":2,"exp":"Muscle tissue is associated with the bones of the skeleton, the heart, and the walls of the hollow organs of the body. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which of the following is a listed function of muscle tissue?","choices":["Produces heat and maintains posture","Transmits impulses and sensations","Stores minerals and lipids","Filters and reabsorbs fluids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Muscle tissue functions include movement, locomotion, maintaining posture, producing heat, facial expressions, pumping blood, and peristalsis. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What are the structural features of skeletal muscle fibers?","choices":["Short, branched, uninucleate cells that display no visible cross-striations at all under the microscope","Flat, scale-shaped cells, each with a single central nucleus, arranged in a continuous sheet","Long, parallel, cylindrical, multinucleate cells with striations (cross stripes) running perpendicular to the cell","Spindle-shaped, single-nucleus cells that display no visible cross-striations under the microscope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Skeletal muscle fibers (cells) are long, parallel, and cylindrical, with many nuclei (multinucleate), and show striations (cross stripes) running perpendicular to the cell, producing voluntary movement. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What movement type does skeletal muscle produce, per the material?","choices":["No movement — only structural support","Peristaltic movement only","Involuntary movement only","Voluntary movement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Skeletal muscle produces voluntary movement, along with locomotion and heat production. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What are the listed functions of nervous tissue?","choices":["Produces movement and body heat through contraction","Regulates and controls body functions, and generates and transmits nerve impulses","Provides structural support to the body and physically connects different tissues","Stores minerals and produces blood cells within its matrix"],"answer":1,"exp":"Nervous tissue regulates and controls body functions, generates and transmits nerve impulses, and supports/insulates/protects impulse-generating neurons. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What are the structural features of a neuron, per the material?","choices":["Cube-shaped cells arranged neatly in a single organized epithelial layer","Branching cells with many long processes and a large central nucleus, transmitting impulses between body areas","Flat, scale-shaped cells with no cellular processes extending outward","Spindle-shaped cells with visible cross-striations along their length"],"answer":1,"exp":"Neurons are branching cells with many long processes and a large central nucleus, transmitting impulses from one area of the body to other areas and regulating activities through neuron impulses. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What are neuroglia (glial cells), and what four types are listed?","choices":["The supportive tissue of the nervous system; astrocytes, microglia, oligodendrocytes, and Schwann cells","A specialized epithelial tissue lining the brain's ventricles; ependymal, ciliated, and two other cell types","A specialized connective tissue located in the brain; fibroblasts, chondrocytes, osteocytes, and adipocytes","The primary impulse-conducting cells; motor neurons, sensory neurons, interneurons, and Purkinje cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Neuroglia (glial cells) are the supportive tissue of the nervous system, including astrocytes, microglia, oligodendrocytes, and Schwann cells. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What are astrocytes, and what is one of their functions?","choices":["Specialized cells that manufacture the myelin sheath within the peripheral nervous system","Branching cells that transmit impulses, each with a single large central nucleus","Star-shaped glial cells, the majority cell type in the CNS, involved in forming the blood-brain barrier","Small cells that migrate through tissue and phagocytose cellular waste products"],"answer":2,"exp":"Astrocytes are star-shaped glial cells that are the majority cell type in the central nervous system, with functions including regulation of the ionic milieu, neurotransmitter uptake/breakdown, and formation of the blood-brain barrier. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What are microglia, and what is their function?","choices":["Specialized cells responsible for producing the myelin sheath within the peripheral nervous system","Small nonneural cells that are migratory and act as phagocytes to waste products of nerve tissue","Specialized cells responsible for producing the myelin sheath within the central nervous system","Star-shaped cells that anchor to capillaries, forming the blood-brain barrier"],"answer":1,"exp":"Microglia are small nonneural cells forming part of the supporting structure of the central nervous system; they are migratory and act as phagocytes to waste products of nerve tissue. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the difference between oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells?","choices":["Oligodendrocytes produce myelin in the peripheral nervous system; Schwann cells produce myelin in the central nervous system","Both produce myelin in the same location","Oligodendrocytes produce myelin in the central nervous system; Schwann cells produce myelin in the peripheral nervous system","Neither cell type produces myelin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Oligodendrocytes provide support to axons and produce the myelin sheath in the central nervous system, while Schwann cells (also called neurilemma cells) produce the myelin sheath in the peripheral nervous system. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which structure is described as containing the cell's genetic material, visible as chromosomes during division and chromatin otherwise?","choices":["Cytoplasm","Nucleolus","Cell membrane","Nucleus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nucleus contains the cell's chromosomes (visible during division) and chromatin (the less condensed form otherwise), among the cell organelles identified in the material. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which cell organelles are identified in the material's overview slide?","choices":["Ribosomes and endoplasmic reticulum only, omitting the nucleus entirely","Cell membrane, cytoplasm, nucleus, nuclear membrane, nucleolus, chromosomes, and chromatin","Only the cell membrane and the surrounding cytoplasm, omitting all internal organelles","Golgi apparatus, mitochondria, and lysosomes only, omitting the nucleus entirely from the list"],"answer":1,"exp":"The material identifies the cell membrane, cytoplasm, nucleus, nuclear membrane, nucleolus, chromosomes, and chromatin as cell organelles to identify. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 55"}]};
